#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""THADD OS · rea — reverse-engineer an artefact down to the byte, then say how you know.

The login banner promises `rea --help`, so this has to be real and it has to work
on a live ISO with no network and no extra packages: everything here is Python
standard library.  Anything that needs a specialist tool (7z, binwalk, unsquashfs,
upx) is used when the image has it and reported as "not installed" when it does not
- the analysis degrades, it never pretends.

    rea <file|dir> [more...]     identify, look for structure, and suggest next steps
    rea --identify <file>        just what this is (magic, format, headers, hashes)
    rea --strings <file>         interesting strings: URLs, keys, paths, commands
    rea --unpack <file>          extract (into ./<name>.unpacked/ by default)
    rea --electron <dir|asar>    unpack an Electron/Chromium app and read its main.js
    rea --dotnet <file>          .NET/CLR assembly: streams, namespaces, entry point
    rea --report <file>          full markdown report for an agent or an examiner
    rea --json <file>            the same facts as JSON

Options
    --out DIR       where to unpack (default: <file>.unpacked next to the artefact)
    --min N         minimum string length (default 6)
    --max-mb N      analyse at most N MiB, to stay fast on disks (default 64)
    --debug         show tracebacks instead of an honest one-line failure
    --version       print the version
    --help          this text

Method (see FORENSIC_SCAN.md): look at the artefact, form a hypothesis, verify it
against the bytes, and only then claim it - the discipline of morluto/rea
("Reverse Engineered Anything", an MCP/agent toolkit).  Upstream `rea-agents`
(MCP server + agent CLI) is installable on demand with `thadd-runtimes rea-agents`;
this tool is the offline, always-present half of the same idea.

Nothing in the artefact is ever executed, mounted or written to.
"""

